\section{Engines}{

The non-spatial fit is a direct Laplace approximation (\code{method = "laplace"})
or a NUTS sampler over the same exact two-state marginal (\code{method = "nuts"},
beta or lognormal cover), giving calibrated intervals and a per-draw
pointwise likelihood for WAIC / LOO. A shared areal field across the
occupancy and cover arms is the \code{method = "nested_laplace"} path (a structured
\code{icar()} / \code{bym2()} term on the psi formula); a structured term on a
non-spatial route errors from the dispatcher with a pointer to it.

\code{method = "nuts"} also samples the coupled areal field(s)
(\code{icar()} / \code{bym2()} / \code{car_proper()}), written either as areal terms or as
the bar \code{spatial(~ 1 || cell, graph = adj)}, TOGETHER WITH their
hyperparameters: the field SD, the mixing (\code{bym2}) or spatial-correlation
(\code{car_proper}) parameter, and - where the formula asks for one - the cover-arm
copy amplitude. Each is bounded to the span the \code{nested_laplace} path's
outer quadrature integrates that axis over, in that grid's own coordinate,
and the same \code{control$sigma.grid} / \code{alpha.grid} / \code{rho.car.grid} knobs set
it on both routes - so the sampler is an independent reference for the
hyperparameter layer rather than a fit conditioned on that layer's point
estimate. The measure over that span is the one the outer grid declares for
the axis: flat in the grid's coordinate for the field SD and the mixing
parameter, and for the copy amplitude the penalized-complexity Exponential
slab, with \code{control$copy.slab = "flat"} asking for the flat alternative the
grid also accepts. The grid weighs that slab against a point mass at
\code{alpha = 0} ("no coupling"), which a gradient sampler cannot visit, so the
sampled copy amplitude is the posterior conditional on a coupled field; the
point mass's share of the prior is read on the \code{nested_laplace} route. \code{fit$nuts$sampled_hyper} and
\code{fit$nuts$fixed_hyper} name which is which per fit (\code{icar} pins \code{rho} at 1:
the intrinsic precision has no mixing parameter; a field with no \code{share()} pins
the copy amplitude at 0), \code{fit$hyper_draws} carries
their posterior alongside \code{field_sd}, the geometric-mean marginal SD the
field implies. \code{control$fixed.hyper = TRUE} conditions on the warm
nested-Laplace estimate instead.

The grid-integrated \code{nested_laplace} path reports \code{field_sd} too
(\code{fit$spatial$field_sd_mean} / \code{field_sd_sd}, alongside \code{sigma_mean}), in
the SAME geo-mean-marginal-SD convention as the NUTS path's \code{field_sd}.
It is a fixed multiple of \code{sigma} (not an independent grid axis), so it is
NOT in \code{fit$means} / \code{fit$vcov} -- folding it in there would make the
joint parameter vcov exactly singular. Do not compare fits, or a fit
against \code{\link[=simulate_occu_cover]{simulate_occu_cover()}}'s \code{sigma}, by reading the raw \code{sigma} on
this path: it is the field's amplitude against the unscaled intrinsic ICAR
precision \code{Q = D - W}, which differs from \code{field_sd}
by \code{sqrt(scale_q)}, a graph-size-dependent factor (about 2.1 for a 30-node
chain graph). \code{field_sd} is the number comparable across fits and to a
simulation truth; \code{sigma} is the raw amplitude the engine's grid axis is
spelled in.

A spatially-varying coefficient - a \emph{weighted} areal term beside the
unweighted intercept field, or a bar column
\code{spatial(~ 1 + year || cell, graph = adj)} - is sampled as a SECOND field
block, with its own whitened surface and its own (SD, mixing, copy amplitude)
coordinates: two fields share no hyperparameter. The surfaces are
\code{fit$spatial_field} and \code{fit$trend_field} / \code{fit$trend_fields} (named by the
weight column), and each block's hypers carry that block's suffix in
\code{fit$hyper_draws} and in \code{fit$nuts$sampled_hyper} (\code{sigma_trend},
\code{alpha_trend}, indexed when there are several). Each field's amplitude is
