\section{What a bare areal term means for the cover arm}{

A term's process is the formula it sits in. An areal term written in the
occurrence formula therefore loads on the OCCURRENCE arm alone: the cover arm
sees no field, and the copy amplitude \code{alpha} is 0. This holds under
\code{method = "nested_laplace"} and \code{method = "nuts"} alike, so the same input
fits the same model on either engine.

To carry the occurrence field onto the cover arm as well, write the copy in
the \code{positive} formula:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{positive = ~ pos_cov + share(spatial())                      # amplitude estimated
positive = ~ pos_cov + share(spatial(), alpha = grid(c(...))) # over given nodes
positive = ~ pos_cov + share(spatial(), alpha = grid(n = 9))  # engine axis, finer
positive = ~ pos_cov + share(spatial(), alpha = 0.5)          # fixed
positive = ~ pos_cov + share(spatial(), prior = list("pc.prec", c(4, 0.01)))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The amplitude is integrated over that axis on the \code{nested_laplace} path and
sampled over its span on the \code{nuts} path; a scalar \verb{alpha =} pins it on both.
\verb{prior =} regularizes the coefficient itself, in the joint driver's
\verb{list(<family>, <params>)} shape (see \code{prior_alpha} in
\code{\link[tulpa:tulpa_nested_laplace_joint]{tulpa::tulpa_nested_laplace_joint()}}); one reaches the engine per fit, so a
fit copying both an intercept and a trend block is refused it rather than
given it on the first (gcol33/tulpa#655).

\code{control$alpha.grid[.trend]} and \code{control$alpha.n[.trend]} are the WIRE
FORMAT these compile into, not user surface: a fit that sets one is refused
and told which formula form to write (gcol33/tulpaObs#295).
\code{control$prior.alpha} is still the lower-level spelling of \verb{prior =}; set it
in one place, not both.

Without a \code{share()} the occurrence field is
NOT carried onto the cover arm -- there is no implicit coupling -- so the
amplitude is not a parameter of the model, and neither engine reports one.
Under \code{nested_laplace} \code{alpha} is absent from \code{coef()}, \code{vcov()} and the
\code{n_params} count; under \code{nuts} it is absent from \code{fit$nuts$sampled_hyper},
\code{fit$nuts$fixed_hyper} and the \code{fit$hyper_draws} columns. \code{predict()} returns
a cover arm the occurrence field does not enter. Add \code{share(spatial())} to
couple the two arms.

The axis the amplitude rides carries prior structure -- a point mass at
\code{alpha = 0} ("no coupling") and a log-spaced slab above it -- so it is set
in one of two ways. \code{share(alpha = grid(...))} STATES its nodes, and with them
that structure. \code{share(alpha = grid(n = 9))} states a RESOLUTION: the engine
re-reads its own axis with that many slab nodes, point mass and slab bounds
unchanged, so sharpening the axis never restates its structure. \verb{terms =}
gives either, per block. The resolution is the only way to raise this axis,
because it does not densify when the donor \code{control$sigma.grid} does: on an
informative data set the outer grid's quadrature effective sample size
saturates on the copy amplitude while every other axis tracks the request
(measured engine-side, \code{NOTES_measurements.md}). One block takes one of the
two; giving both is an error. \code{control$alpha.grid[.trend]} and
\code{control$alpha.n[.trend]} are the wire format these compile into and are
refused as user input (gcol33/tulpaObs#295).

Set it when the fit's hyperparameter intervals are reported. On the coupled
SBC fixture the declared resolution leaves the field SD miscalibrated once
the data are informative -- at 10 visits per site its uniformity p-value is
9.1e-05, against 0.17 at 3 visits -- and \code{control$alpha.n = 21} returns every
scored quantity to nominal at both. Thirteen slab nodes do not (the field SD
still reads 4.5e-03), and the price is a 2.3-3x longer fit, since the outer
grid is a tensor. Point estimates are not affected the way the intervals are;
the measurement is in \code{NOTES_measurements.md}.
}

\section{Coupled fields and spatially-varying trends}{

The spatial engines (\code{method = "nested_laplace"}, default
\code{control$engine = "joint"}, and \code{method = "nuts"}) share one areal field (the
cell intercept, an unweighted \code{icar()} / \code{bym2()} term) across the occupancy
and cover arms, when the \code{positive} formula copies it. ADDITIONAL coupled
fields - spatially-varying coefficients,
e.g. a temporal trend - are added as \emph{weighted} areal terms in the formula:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{~ elev + icar(graph = adj) + icar(graph = adj, weight = year)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Each weighted term \code{icar(graph, weight = col)} is a second field whose
contribution to a predictor row is \code{weight_i * z[cell_i]}. All such fields
share the same graph; a \code{share()} carries one onto the cover arm with its own
scale (\code{alpha} for the intercept field, \code{alpha_trend} for a trend field),
integrated over the outer grid, and a field the \code{positive} formula does not
copy stays on occurrence. \code{share(spatial(), terms = list(...))} gives a
per-block amplitude and must address every block. The intercept field is
reported in \code{fit$spatial_field}, trend fields in \code{fit$trend_field} /
\code{fit$trend_fields}. The trend coupling grid defaults to
the intercept block's amplitude; give the trend its own with
\code{share(spatial(), terms = list(intercept = ..., trend = ...))}. Under
\code{method = "nuts"} each field is its own sampled block, with the scales
sampled over those same spans rather than integrated over the grid.

A single trend field may also be requested out-of-band with
\code{control = list(trend = list(weight = "<col>"))}, naming a numeric per-cell
covariate in the cell \code{data}; this is the equivalent of one weighted formula
term. Specify the trend field one way or the other, not both.
}

\section{Sites larger than cells (\code{group_var})}{

By default each site (one row of \code{y} / \code{data}, one latent occupancy state) is
its own field node, so the graph must have one node per site. Passing
\code{group_var = "<col>"} to the \code{icar()} / \code{bym2()} term maps each site to a
field node named by that integer column, so several sites can share one node.
The field then stays length \code{n_cells} (the graph) while occupancy, detection,
and cover run over \code{n_sites}. The motivating layout is a site = cell x
time-period: plots in a cell-period are the detection replicates, occupancy is
per cell-period, and a per-site time weight on a coupled trend field
(\code{icar(graph, weight = time, group_var = "cell")}) gives a detection-corrected
occupancy trend on a shared cell field. Supported on the default
\code{joint} engine; the \code{v2_joint} / \code{v3_nested} escape hatches bind the
field 1:1 to sites and reject \code{group_var}.
}

\section{Per-group random intercept on the shared-field path (\code{re()} / \code{(1 | g)})}{

On the spatial \code{nested_laplace} path a single random INTERCEPT term on the psi
formula -- \code{re(g)} or the \code{(1 | g)} bar -- layers a per-group occupancy offset
on top of the shared field. It joins the joint fit as one \code{iid} prior block
whose latent rides the occupancy arm only; its variance integrates on the
outer grid alongside the field sigma / alpha and is reported as the \code{sigma_re}
hyperparameter, with the per-group BLUPs in \code{fit$re} and via \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}}. A
default \code{re.sigma.grid} (log-spaced) sets the grid; pass \code{control$re.sigma.grid}
to override. Scope: one random-intercept term (a scalar per group maps onto the
one iid block); a random slope, a correlated multi-coefficient block, or an RE
without a shared field is rejected (the joint engine integrates every variance
component on its grid, so multiple variance components do not scale -- richer
RE is the non-spatial cover-hurdle EM's route). This is the single-species
analogue of the community spatial occu_cover (\code{\link[=ms_occu_cover]{ms_occu_cover()}})
and the tulpaObs consumer of tulpa's field + per-group RE engine composition.

A bar is therefore always a random effect, never a spatial field, even when the
grouping factor names the areal graph nodes. The engine's inline-MCAR call
\code{tulpa::spatial(graph, ~ 1 + x | cell)} reads \verb{| cell} as a separable spatial
field, but the same spelling in an \code{occu_cover()} formula is an IID random
effect on \code{cell}. For a spatial field on the cells use an areal term --
\code{icar(graph = adj, group_var = "cell")} (plus
\code{icar(graph = adj, weight = x, group_var = "cell")} for a spatially-varying
trend) -- not a bar. When a formula carries a bar whose grouping factor is also
an areal term's \code{group_var}, \code{occu_cover()} emits a one-time message noting the
bar is fitted as a random effect; suppress it with \code{\link[base:suppressMessages]{base::suppressMessages()}}.
}

\section{Independent field on the cover arm (placement)}{

A \code{share(spatial())} in the \code{positive} formula shares the occupancy field: the
cover arm sees it as \verb{alpha * (occupancy field)}, the coregionalization copy
on the outer \verb{(sigma, alpha)} grid. When the cover trend is spatially
structured but is not
a scalar multiple of the occupancy field, \code{alpha} collapses toward 0 and the
cover arm inherits no field, so per-cell conditional cover (and its change over
time, \code{delta_cover_cond}) comes out flat. A \code{spatial()} bar placed in the
\code{positive} formula adds an INDEPENDENT, non-copied areal field on the cover arm
alone:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{tobs(occurrence = ~ x + icar(graph = adj, group_var = "cell"),
     detection  = ~ 1,
     positive   = ~ time + spatial(~ 1 + time || cell, graph = adj),
     data = cell_dat, y = y, y_pos = y_pos,
     family = occu_cover("lognormal"), method = "nested_laplace")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The occupancy field still drives psi and, via the alpha copy, \code{delta_cover_exp};
the independent cover field carries a cover-specific structure the alpha copy
cannot express. Its intercept + coefficient columns become separate ICAR blocks
on the cover arm, each with its own precision integrated on the outer grid and
reported as \code{sigma_pos_field} (intercept) / \verb{sigma_pos_field_<col>} (a covariate
column). The field SD grid defaults to \code{control$sigma.grid}; set
\code{control$sigma.grid.pos.field} to integrate it over its own (usually coarser)
grid, which keeps the added axis from multiplying the outer-grid cost.
The per-cell field posterior is in \code{fit$pos_field} / \code{fit$pos_field_table}
(and \code{fit$pos_field_tables} per column). Scope: it composes with the shared
occupancy field but uses per-visit cover (\code{cover_aggregate = "none"}) and does
not combine with the correlated \code{|} MCAR field, the latent cover RE, or the
batched fused path; like the shared field it is fitted as ICAR (bym2/car read as
ICAR). A static intercept cover field is only weakly identified against the
occupancy field's alpha copy (both are per-cell cover offsets); a time-weighted
trend cover field is identified separately and is what makes \code{delta_cover_cond}
spatially varying.

The same placement works on the detection arm: a spatial-field term in the
\code{detection} formula (\code{detection = ~ 1 + spatial(~ 0 + time || cell, graph = adj)}) fits an independent field on the detection predictor, for a
spatially-structured detection probability. Its SD is reported as
\code{sigma_p_field} / \verb{sigma_p_field_<col>}.
}

\section{Checkpoint / resume}{

A full-field \code{occu_cover()} fit integrates over a large outer hyperparameter
grid and can run for hours, so a reboot or OOM kill otherwise loses the whole
run. \code{control$checkpoint = list(path = "fit.ckpt", resume = TRUE)} makes the
joint engine append each completed grid cell to \code{path}; a \code{resume = TRUE} run
loads the finished cells and solves only the remaining ones, reproducing the
from-scratch fit. \code{resume = FALSE} starts a fresh file (any stale checkpoint at
\code{path} is removed first). A torn tail from a killed write is discarded and
re-solved, and a checkpoint written for different data or settings is rejected
rather than resumed onto. Forwarded to \code{\link[tulpa:tulpa_nested_laplace_joint]{tulpa::tulpa_nested_laplace_joint()}}.
}

\section{Cell-aggregated cover (\code{cover_aggregate})}{

On the shared-field spatial path the cover arm has one observation per
detected visit, so a cell with many detected plots informs the shared field
far more than the single occupancy observation for that cell; the field is
then driven almost entirely by the cover arm and the detection-corrected
occupancy surface flattens. \code{cover_aggregate} collapses the cover arm to a
single response per occupancy unit (cell-period) so the two arms inform the
field with comparable weight:
\itemize{
\item \code{"mean"} -- model the mean cover over the unit's detected visits;
\item \code{"median"} -- model the median cover;
\item \code{"latent"} -- model a per-unit cover random effect
\eqn{u_i \sim N(0, \sigma_u^2)} shared across the unit's detected visits
and integrated out per unit, so the cover arm contributes one marginal
observation per unit while keeping every detected visit (the principled
counterpart of mean / median aggregation). The lognormal arm integrates
in closed form (compound-symmetry); the beta arm uses adaptive
Gauss-Hermite quadrature (\code{control$n.quad}, default 15). The within-unit
dispersion is pre-fit from the within-unit spread and held fixed;
\eqn{\sigma_u} is integrated on the outer grid (\code{control$sigma.u.grid}).
\item \code{"none"} -- the per-visit cover arm (one cover observation per
detected visit).
}
\code{NULL} (the default) selects \code{"mean"} on the shared-field spatial
\code{nested_laplace} path and \code{"none"} on the non-spatial \code{laplace} path (which
has no shared field to over-weight). Aggregation needs a cell-level positive
design: a visit-level covariate in the \code{positive} formula cannot be collapsed
to one value per cell and errors. Aggregation is currently wired on the
spatial path only; requesting \code{"mean"} / \code{"median"} on the non-spatial
\code{laplace} fit errors rather than silently using per-visit cover.
}

\section{Random effects on the observation arms}{

Under \code{method = "nested_laplace"} (shared-field spatial) and \code{method = "nuts"}
(non-spatial, which samples the group SD), random
intercepts may be written on the \strong{detection} or \strong{positive-cover} formula
with the usual \code{lme4} bar or \code{re()} spelling, e.g.
\code{detection = ~ effort + (1 | habitat)}. The grouping is per visit -- one code
per \verb{(site, visit)} entry, distinct from the per-site occupancy-arm
grouping -- so a many-level categorical visit covariate (an EUNIS
habitat class, an observer) enters as a partially pooled random intercept.
\strong{Crossed} (\code{(1 | habitat) + (1 | observer)}) and \strong{nested}
(\code{(1 | region/site)}, which expands to \code{re(region) + re(region:site)})
groupings are supported: each term joins the joint fit as its own \code{iid} latent
block. Each block's variance integrates on the outer grid (\code{sigma_re_p} /
\code{sigma_re_pos} for a lone term on the detection / cover arm, suffixed by the
grouping variable -- \code{sigma_re_p_habitat} -- when several terms share an arm;
tune with \code{control$re.sigma.grid.p} / \code{re.sigma.grid.pos}). The per-group BLUPs
are reported in \code{fit$re} (one entry per term, keyed by arm or \code{"<arm>:<var>"})
and via \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}}. \code{predict()} sums every term's BLUP offset on the predicted
arm when \code{newdata} carries the grouping columns and \code{type = "detection"} (or
\code{"occurrence"} / \code{"cover_cond"}); an unseen / held-out level shrinks that term
to the population mean. Several crossed terms multiply the outer grid, so set
\code{control$integration = "ccd"} (and/or coarsen the RE grids) at scale.

Random \strong{slopes} are also supported (tulpa engine >= 0.0.39): an uncorrelated
slope (\code{(x || g)}, \code{(0 + x | g)}) is one per-row weighted \code{iid} block per
coefficient, and a correlated slope (\code{(1 + x | g)}) one multivariate
free-Sigma \code{miid} block. A slope term's \code{fit$re} entry carries an
\verb{[n_groups x n_coefs]} BLUP matrix, a per-coefficient \code{sigma}, and (correlated)
a \code{cor} matrix; the hyperparameters are \verb{sigma_re_p_<coef>} and
\verb{cor_re_p_<ci>_<cj>}, marginalized over the grid. \code{predict()} weights each
coefficient by its covariate column in \code{newdata} (intercept = 1). Each slope
covariate is \strong{standardized} to unit SD before fitting (mirroring the
fixed-effect design), so the variance grid is scale-invariant -- the reported
slope BLUPs and \code{sigma} are back-transformed to the covariate's natural units
(correlation is scale-free). A correlated slope adds \code{p(p+1)/2} Sigma axes to
the outer grid, so its free-Sigma grid uses a compact principled default
(symmetric correlation nodes including 0); widen it with
\code{control$re.logchol.grid.p} / \code{re.logchol.grid.pos}.

The positive-cover RE needs per-visit cover (\code{cover_aggregate = "none"}). As
with the occupancy-arm RE, each grid-integrated variance carries the binary /
small-cluster inner-Laplace attenuation and is a lower bound on the truth; the
BLUPs recover the per-group structure.

\code{method = "nuts"} samples an observation-arm random \strong{intercept} instead of
integrating it on a grid: each grouping factor is a non-centered block
(\verb{b_g = sigma_re z_g}, \code{z_g ~ N(0, 1)}) whose group SD is a coordinate of the
sampled vector under a \code{N(0, 1.5^2)} prior on \verb{log sigma_re} -- the same
weakly informative width the other observation-family samplers use, and the
reason the sampled SD carries no small-cluster attenuation to correct for.
Crossed and nested groupings are simply several blocks, on either or both
observation arms. \code{fit$re} keys and \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}} match the \code{nested_laplace}
path; each term additionally reports \code{sigma_median} and the per-draw
\code{sigma_draws}, the summaries to quote for a right-skewed variance component,
with per-SD convergence in \code{fit$nuts$re_sigma_rhat} / \code{re_sigma_ess}. The
sampled coefficient surface (\code{coef()}, \code{vcov()}, \code{fit$draws}) is unchanged.
Still on \code{nested_laplace} only: a random slope, and an observation-arm RE
composed with the coupled areal field. \code{waic()} / \code{cpo()} on a
sampled fit read the coefficient draws, so they score the RE at zero -- the
same limitation the sampled coupled field has.
}

